bodyFormat:string = "and-v2"

body:prose = >`
  &ND v2

  # Aeonic Civil Date Anchoring v1 Working Contract [# civil-anchor-working-contract]

  Status: normative proposal-stage mutable working contract, 2026-11-19.
  Publication is disabled. Implementations MUST satisfy the
  [@ /artifacts/assets/aeonic-operations/v1/civil-anchor-contract.schema.json | closed working schema]
  and the semantics, admission and diagnostic rules below. There is no immutable
  anchoring snapshot or stable conformance claim yet. Existing frozen resources,
  other capability requirements and their defaults remain unchanged.

  ## 1. Domain and capability [# civil-anchor-section-1-domain-and-capability]

  The profile is [$ aeonic.operations.temporal.civil-anchor.v1].
  The complete capability is [$ Aeonic.Operations.Temporal.CivilAnchor], requiring [$ anchor].
  Selection is explicit through operation policy or reusable context. No operand
  shape, host date, locale, timezone or document metadata selects it automatically.
  Validate both supplied configurations before resolving policy over context.

  The complete capability requires all admitted anchor/clock forms and the shared
  metadata, admission, accounting and diagnostic rules. There are no semantic case
  opt-outs. It does not require implementing date, clock or date-time arithmetic;
  consuming/producing those value bundles is a separate requirement from producing
  them through another operation. Availability is trusted host configuration.

  One exact calendar day anchors the clock's reference day zero. Reduced-date and
  multi-date anchors are ineligible. Date-wise broadcasting, Cartesian products,
  date/clock pairing expressions, wrapping/projection, inverse unanchoring,
  timezone/WTC/leap/UTC realization and elapsed-time authority are outside scope.

  ## 2. Closed logical request [# civil-anchor-section-2-closed-logical-request]

  ~~~$ json
  {
    "operation": "anchor",
    "input": {
      "anchor": { "temporal": { "category": "civilDate", "value": "2024-02-28" } },
      "clock": {
        "temporal": {
          "category": "civilClockSet",
          "pieces": [{ "kind": "point", "at": { "day": "1", "time": "01:30:00" } }]
        },
        "originClockForm": "second",
        "history": "none-declared"
      },
      "context": { "profile": "aeonic.operations.temporal.civil-anchor.v1" }
    }
  }
  ~~~$

  Request has exactly [$ operation] and [$ input]; input requires [$ anchor] and [$ clock]
  and optionally admits [$ context] and [$ policy]. Context/policy each have only an
  optional nonempty scalar-string [$ profile]. A selected profile is required; there
  are no calendar-unit, count, rounding, output-family or clock-form settings.
  Unknown fields fail closed. Trusted limits/capability availability are outside
  logical input; logical fields cannot increase them.

  ### Date anchor [# civil-anchor-section-date-anchor]

  Reuse civil-date snapshot 0.1 bundles: authored/materialized [$ civilDate] and
  canonical derived [$ civilDateSet]. Authored metadata may be omitted; default origin
  from the literal shape and clamping history to [$ none-declared]. Derived sets
  require [$ originGranularity] and [$ history]. Preserve valid supplied metadata.

  Parse and validate all supplied supported date data, including intrinsic Gregorian
  validity and canonical nonempty inclusive ranges. Reduced literals are valid
  civil-date operands, not silently filled-in dates. Eligibility is checked later:
  an authored literal must cover one day; a derived set must have exactly one range
  whose start equals its end. Origin metadata cannot satisfy that test. Empty,
  overlapping, adjacent, unsorted or malformed derived ranges are not repaired.

  ### Clock [# civil-anchor-section-clock]

  Reuse civil-clock snapshot 0.1 bundles: authored/materialized [$ civilClock] and
  canonical derived [$ civilClockSet]. Authored forms are [$ HH:], [$ HH:MM] and
  [$ HH:MM:SS\\[.fraction\\]] on reference day zero. Infer omitted authored origin from
  the form, and default history to [$ none-declared]. Derived sets require
  [$ originClockForm] and [$ history]; do not infer either from their endpoints.

  Derived day text is canonical signed integer text, with no plus, leading zeroes
  or negative zero. Time is full canonical [$ HH:MM:SS\\[.fraction\\]], with no trailing
  fractional zeroes. Points and positive-width intervals form a nonempty canonical
  sorted set. Endpoint flags, gaps and relative days have their existing meanings.
  Seconds are 00–59; offsets, [$ Z], [$ &], dates, [$ 24:00] and second 60 are not supported
  clock literal spellings here. No precision grid or implicit rounding is introduced.

  Recognized other value families are deferred. Validate their enclosing bundle's
  applicable supplied metadata, but leave descriptor payload opaque before the
  stage 5 [$ unsupported_domain] result. Unknown category tags are [$ invalid_operand].
  Logical JSON admission still reads/measures the request, including deferred data.

  ## 3. Exact translation and domain [# civil-anchor-section-3-exact-translation-and-domain]

  Let A be the anchor's zero-based Gregorian ordinal: 0001-01-01 has A=0. Translate
  clock coordinate [$ {day:d,time:t}] to the civil date at ordinal A+d with the exact
  same clock coordinate t. Relative day zero means midnight-to-midnight of the
  selected anchor day. Signed carry/borrow is applied exactly once; never wrap it
  away, clamp it, select another anchor or consult the current date/timezone.

  This is a strictly order-preserving translation of the complete clock set.
  Translate each point or interval directly and retain both endpoint flags. Canonical
  ordering, gaps and nonmergeability are preserved; successful output has exactly
  P pieces for P clock pieces. A hundred-day interval remains one interval. Do not
  enumerate its dates, seconds or fractional grid or substitute a containing period.

  The result domain is the existing civil-date-time domain, years 0001–9999. Every
  covered coordinate must lie in that domain. Reject the whole result if any member
  falls outside; no clipping, subset removal, partial success or error value.
  An interval's excluded end exactly at 10000-01-01T00:00:00 becomes
  [$ {"boundary":"endOfDomain"}]. That boundary cannot be a point, start or included
  end. An excluded end beyond it still implies out-of-domain members and fails.

  Compare signed relative-day magnitudes with the bounded legal ordinal offsets
  before calendar conversion or constructing date-time text. Huge admitted day text
  can establish [$ unrepresentable_result] compactly; no expanded seconds quantity or
  day enumeration is needed. Fractions are copied exactly, not scaled into an epoch
  integer. This contract adds no numeric-coefficient/exponent/workspace dimension.

  ## 4. Closed success and metadata [# civil-anchor-section-4-closed-success-and-metadata]

  ~~~$ json
  {
    "outcome": "value",
    "value": {
      "temporal": {
        "category": "civilDateTimeSet",
        "pieces": [{ "kind": "point", "at": "2024-02-29T01:30:00" }]
      },
      "originDateGranularity": "day",
      "originClockForm": "second",
      "history": { "clamping": "none-declared", "approximation": "none-declared" }
    },
    "metadata": {
      "evaluation": "exact-for-policy",
      "clamping": "not-applied",
      "effectivePolicy": { "profile": "aeonic.operations.temporal.civil-anchor.v1" }
    }
  }
  ~~~$

  Success has exactly [$ outcome], [$ value] and [$ metadata]; metadata has exactly the
  three shown fields. [$ effectivePolicy] contains only the selected anchor profile.
  The complete value is an existing [$ civilDateTimeSet] bundle. Do not claim that the
  whole anchoring response matches another profile's operation-result envelope.

  Map date [$ originGranularity] to result [$ originDateGranularity]; copy clock
  [$ originClockForm]. Copy date history into [$ history.clamping] and clock history into
  [$ history.approximation]. Preserve declared markers and undeclared origins, even
  when the result looks like a complete day/second form. Anchoring introduces no
  new clamp or approximation, and detailed provenance remains deferred.

  Applications can pass the returned [$ value] directly to an existing date-time
  operation, explicitly selecting that operation's profile/context. Do not pass the
  entire response or silently carry the anchoring profile into another evaluator.
  Materialization remains a separate existing operation with explicit selectors.

  ## 5. Portable accounting and selected allowances [# civil-anchor-section-5-portable-accounting-and-selected-allowances]

  Let R be the supplied date-range count and P the clock-piece count. An authored
  date/clock has one implicit range/piece; a reduced date literal still counts as
  one range before eligibility. Required work is R+P; required intermediate capacity
  is P; successful output count is P. Count points and intervals equally, with no
  per-endpoint or per-covered-day multiplier. Optimized implementations use the same
  admission counts and cannot shortcut an ineligible anchor around stage 6 guards.

  The mutable CTS selects the following calibration map; implementations MUST expose the same independently configurable dimensions. These are not host defaults or verified support floors:

  | Limit | Selected allowance | Meaning |
  | <-- | <-- | <-- |
  | maxRequestBytes | 65536 | Actual compact logical JSON UTF-8 bytes, or verbatim text bytes. |
  | maxRequestDepth | 32 | Logical JSON container depth. |
  | maxTemporalCharacters | 1024 | Each date/clock text payload or endpoint time text, counted as Unicode scalars before grammar. |
  | maxInputRanges | 128 | Date anchor's conceptual/supplied ranges; before range-element traversal. |
  | maxInputPieces | 512 | Clock's conceptual/supplied pieces; before derived piece traversal. |
  | maxRelativeDayDigits | 256 | Each clock-coordinate day magnitude, excluding a minus sign; zero costs one digit. |
  | maxFractionDigits | 256 | Each supplied clock fraction, before trimming authored trailing zeroes. |
  | maxWork | 16384 | R+P logical work units. |
  | maxIntermediatePieces | 4096 | P translated candidates, checked before eligibility/construction. |
  | maxOutputPieces | 512 | P successful date-time pieces, after domain checks. |
  | maxResultBytes | 131072 | Complete compact successful JSON envelope, before final text rendering/serialization. |
  |~ Anchoring selected resource allowances

  These are selected CTS allowances, not semantic ceilings or verified native
  capacities. Every dimension requires its own admission/capacity evidence and
  explicit companions; no Cartesian-product guarantee follows. Known nonnegative
  safe-integer trusted settings are required; malformed/unknown settings are harness
  configuration errors. No separate bare-text output, numerical amount or count
  limit is needed because this operation produces only a derived temporal set.

  All limits are per operation. Whole-chain policy belongs to the application.
  Native JSON graph/shared-subtree admission must remain bounded by logical bytes
  and depth with cycle rejection. Arbitrary accessors/proxies/subclasses, eager key
  enumeration, outer STDIO framing, process heap/disk, browser preparation/display
  and host timeouts remain separately qualified host/application concerns.

  ## 6. Deterministic diagnostics [# civil-anchor-section-6-deterministic-diagnostics]

  Implementations MUST stop at the first failed substep in this seven-stage order.

  | Stage | Order and required behavior |
  | <-- | <-- |
  | 1 | Scalar text admission, logical byte/depth guards before deeper decoding; [$ invalid_operand] or reached [$ resource_limit], null path. |
  | 2 | Strict JSON/duplicate keys; request required fields and closure; input object/anchor/clock/context/policy vocabulary; operation/capability availability; known-operation anchor/clock arity. [$ invalid_operand] or [$ unsupported_operation]. |
  | 3 | Context then policy shape/closure, supplied profile value validity, then effective profile availability. [$ invalid_policy] or [$ unsupported_profile]. |
  | 4 | Validate anchor bundle first, then clock bundle, using their frozen operand rules with rebased paths and the new profile's applicable allowances. [$ invalid_operand] or reached [$ resource_limit]. |
  | 5 | Reject recognized deferred families, anchor before clock, with [$ unsupported_domain] at the category path. |
  | 6 | Guard R+P work first, then P intermediate capacity; check anchor covers one day ([$ incompatible_operand] at [$ /input/anchor/temporal]); translate compactly. Reached budgets use [$ resource_limit], null path. |
  | 7 | Check complete translated coverage/domain first ([$ unrepresentable_result], null path), then output-piece allowance and complete successful-envelope bytes ([$ resource_limit], null path). Render only after admission. |
  |~ Anchoring validation and diagnostic precedence

  Within-stage conventions are normative: require fields in
  listed order; then reject unknown fields in Unicode scalar-name order; no null
  alias for omission. Context and policy are both validated even if policy overrides
  context. Missing selection uses [$ /input/policy/profile]. Required request fields
  are operation/input; required anchor-operation input fields are anchor/clock.
  Unavailable/unknown operation uses [$ /operation] before its arity failures.

  Anchor metadata order is required derived origin/history, bundle closure, history,
  origin, then descriptor fields and payload. Authored anchor's conceptual range
  guard precedes detailed date payload parsing; supported date text then has type,
  character allowance and intrinsic grammar/validity checks. Derived date ranges
  must be a nonempty array before length admission, then validate start/end in order
  before range canonicality. Clock metadata/coordinate/piece suborder follows the
  frozen clock binding, including authored clock validity before its implicit
  piece/day capacities. Empty derived arrays are invalid before their length guard.

  Diagnostics have exactly [$ outcome], [$ reason], [$ context] and optionally a
  human-readable scalar-string [$ message]. Context has exactly integer [$ stage] and
  [$ path] (RFC6901 pointer or null). No diagnostic includes [$ value] or success
  metadata. Recognized reasons are the existing invalid_operand, invalid_policy,
  unsupported_operation, unsupported_profile, unsupported_domain, resource_limit,
  incompatible_operand and unrepresentable_result. Unknown logical fields fail;
  duplicate JSON keys and raw numerical spellings remain admission evidence.
  Text and structured CTS-token ingress preserve supplied raw number spellings;
  native host JSON values do not recover spellings lost before the boundary.


  ## 7. Admission details and vocabulary [# civil-anchor-section-7-admission-details-and-vocabulary]

  The request's generic input vocabulary is exactly anchor, clock, context, policy.
  At stage 2 require request operation then input; reject unknown request fields;
  require input object and reject unknown input fields; validate operation as a
  nonempty scalar string and test operation availability; then require anchor,
  clock in that order. Unsupported operation uses /operation. Missing fields use
  their exact JSON Pointer; nonobject request/input uses null or /input respectively.
  A valid but unavailable capability is an unsupported_operation, never a smaller
  semantic opt-out. All supplied configurations are checked at stage 3: context
  object/closure then policy object/closure, context profile then policy profile,
  then effective profile availability. Invalid configurations use invalid_policy
  at the reached field; missing selection is invalid_policy at /input/policy/profile.
  An unavailable effective profile uses unsupported_profile at its selected field.

  Stage 4 begins each bundle by requiring temporal as an object, then a recognized
  scalar-string category. Use date metadata on the anchor and clock metadata on
  the clock even for a deferred family. Only civilDate/civilDateSet are supported
  anchor categories and only civilClock/civilClockSet are supported clock categories.
  Recognized category vocabulary in this new profile is:
  [$ civilDate], [$ civilDateSet], [$ civilClock], [$ civilClockSet], [$ civilDateTime], [$ civilDateTimeSet], [$ finiteNumber], [$ exactNumber], [$ exactRational], [$ string], [$ boolean], [$ symbol], [$ positiveInfinity], [$ negativeInfinity], [$ nan], [$ explicitNull], [$ explicitAbsence], [$ toggle], [$ hex], [$ radix], [$ encoding], [$ separator], [$ sansaAddress], [$ referenceForm], [$ temporal], [$ container].
  Deferred descriptor fields remain opaque, including unknown descriptor fields;
  their containing bundle remains closed and any supplied metadata is validated.
  The three frozen value contracts are normative for supported bundle validation,
  with operand paths rebased under /input/anchor or /input/clock and only the
  applicable anchoring allowances applied. The new profile adds no numeric amount,
  count, numerical workspace or materialized-text admission requirements.

  Date authored payload checks type then maxTemporalCharacters before exact date
  grammar and intrinsic validity, allowing only YYYY-, YYYY-MM or YYYY-MM-DD.
  Each derived date endpoint checks the same text allowance before full-date
  syntax and Gregorian validity. Years are 0001–9999. For range objects require
  start/end, reject unknown fields, parse start then end, reject inversion at end,
  then reject noncanonical neighboring ranges at the offending start. Range guard
  paths are /input/anchor/temporal for authored forms and
  /input/anchor/temporal/ranges for derived forms. Text and intrinsic failures use
  the reached value/start/end field. Calendar eligibility is not an input grammar
  restriction: valid month/year literals MUST reach stage 6.

  For clocks, require temporal then category; derived bundles require originClockForm
  then history, before closure; validate history then origin, descriptor required
  fields and closure, then payload. A derived nonempty pieces array is admitted
  against maxInputPieces before iterating it. Piece fields are kind then at for a
  point, or start/end/includeStart/includeEnd for an interval. Require and reject
  unknown fields before parsing coordinates; parse at/start then end, then flags,
  interval order and cross-piece canonicality. Coordinates require day/time and
  closure; day type, magnitude-character allowance, canonical syntax; then time
  type, scalar-character allowance, grammar, fractional allowance, canonical fraction,
  intrinsic clock ranges. Relative-day allowance excludes at most one initial minus;
  a plus is not removed. Authored intrinsic clock validity precedes its implicit
  day capacity, then implicit piece capacity, both one. Authored fraction capacity
  counts supplied digits before trimming trailing zeroes. Fraction failures use
  the time/value path; derived piece allowance uses the pieces path; implicit
  capacities use temporal. Reversed/zero-width intervals use the end path; a
  noncanonical neighbor uses its at or start field. All ordering is exact.

  Stage 1 uses actual compact logical JSON UTF-8 bytes, with minimal JSON string
  escaping and no insignificant whitespace for structured requests; text requests
  use supplied bytes verbatim. Preserve every raw numerical token in structured
  CTS logical requests, including integer -0 and huge exponents in deferred payloads.
  Do not reconstruct spellings already lost by a caller before native admission.
  Container depth counts the root container as one; scalar children do not increase
  it. Guard Unicode-scalar text, bytes and depth with bounded incremental scanning
  before strict JSON decoding at stage 2. Do not materialize a deferred descriptor
  or an exponentially unfolded shared graph merely to reject its logical size.
  Reject cyclic native graphs and duplicate logical object keys. Scalar text failure
  is invalid_operand at stage 1, null path; invalid JSON/duplicates are invalid_operand
  at stage 2, null path. Host framing is outside these logical measurements.

  Trusted resource settings are nonnegative safe JSON integers, not Booleans, null,
  fractional values or unknown names. Malformed settings fail as harness errors,
  not logical diagnostic results. Omission selects host defaults; the CTS always
  sends the complete calibrated map, including case-local overrides. The complete
  successful result byte limit excludes optional protocol framing. Measure exact
  compact UTF-8 size before final serialization, using bounded fragments; do not
  construct an oversized whole string to discover its failure. Diagnostic envelopes
  are not subject to successful-result byte limits.

  ## 8. Reuse authority and mutable conformance [# civil-anchor-section-8-reuse-authority-and-mutable-conformance]

  Supported value semantics are reused from civil-date, civil-clock and civil
  date-time specification snapshots 0.1, available in the isolated source/schema
  resources under /artifacts/assets/aeonic-operations/v1/snapshots/0.1/.
  The new closed working schema embeds only the necessary definitions, with local
  reference prefixes; it does not change any frozen resource. The date and clock
  deferred-category enumerations are extended for this profile only. Schema validity
  cannot establish Gregorian intrinsic validity, set canonicality, exact translation,
  explicit profile availability, deterministic diagnostics or bounded admission.

  The complete mutable CTS lane is aeonic-temporal-civil-anchor. Every case is
  mandatory for its one capability; there is no arbitrary case filter. Artifact
  validation, synthetic fixture replay, actual implementation execution, native
  capacity qualification, immutable capture and releases are separate facts.
  Mutable working executions never establish stable snapshot conformance. An
  anchoring-only provider need not implement downstream date-time operations;
  returned value bundles must nevertheless conform to the existing date-time format.
`
